Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) usually have micropores smaller than $2\mathsf{n m}$ , which may restrict their applications in some cases. Hierarchical-pore MOFs (H-MOFs), are a new family of MOF materials, possessing both micro- and mesopores to address this problem. Here we demonstrate a competitive coordination strategy to synthesis of H-MOF nanostructures, such as two-dimensional (2D) H-MOF nanosheets and H-MOF nanocubes, evolving through an etching process tuned by a competitive ligand. The as-synthesized 2D H-MOF nanosheets can serve as a substrate to in situ immobilize Pd nanoparticles to achieve surfactant-free Pd catalyst, by means of a simple soaking method of $\mathsf{P d}^{2+}$ precursors. Combining with the unique structures and gas adsorption capacity of H-MOF-5, the Pd-H-MOF-5 catalyst exhibits a superior catalytic performance.
## Introduction
Constructed by inorganic nodes and organic linkers with two or more ligation sites, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have received tremendous attention in recent years1-3. Their glamorous diversities in structures could be tailored by rational design of metal-based inorganic nodes and organic linkers with judicious geometrical and functional selections. Traditional smhorwenanwdidemsopre andewpotMenOtFisal bauprgpleicoantiinogn4,s 5,i nangdasthsetyo rahgaev6e, adsorption7, 8, molecular separation9, catalysis10-12, imaging1 13 and so on. Notably, two-dimensional (2D) MOFs, with many highly active sites on their surfaces like other 2D materials14-16, have attracted increasing interest recently17-19. Besides, due to their large surface areas, guest molecules could effectively pass through the micropores18, which could benefit gasinvolved catalytic reactions.
Meanwhile, MOFs, owing to their microporosity (pore size $\rvert<$ $2\mathsf{n m})$ , benefit the adsorption and separation of small molecules. However, the small pore size in microporous MOFs in turn limits fast mass diffusion and restrains larger molecules from entering into the center through MOF micropores, hence restricts the potential practical applications in some cases20, 21. Therefore, enlarging the pore size or creating some larger pores in primitive MOF structures is necessary, however, challenging at present. Once introducing mesopores into the previously microporous MOF structures, the as-achieved hierarchical-pore MOFs (H-MOFs) would be advantageous in utterly utilizing of materials22, 23. The micropores provide exceptionally high surface areas and large pore volumes, whereas the mesopores facilitate diffusion and accessibility. Many efforts have been made to reach the aim. The most commonly adopted one is the ligand-extension method24 - by using longer organic ligands to achieve the mesoporosity - and quite a few MOFs with ultralarge pores have been elaborately designed and synthesized25. However, it is usually limited by unstability and possible interpenetration as the size extends, along with the high cost and difficulty in synthesizing larger organic ligands. Besides, surfactant-templating method22, microwave-assisted method26, CO2-expanded liquid route27, ionic liquid system28, post-synthetic method29 are also alternative to create mesopores. Nonetheless, these innovative methods require either a template or complicated techniques, and are difficult to extend to other systems. So new versatile methods are imperative to establish stable and high-quality H-MOFs.
Herein, we developed a competitive coordination strategy to synthesis of H-MOF nanostructures, possessing both microand mesopores in the network. The as-synthesized 2D H-MOF nanosheets and H-MOF nanocubes evolved through an etching process tuned by a competitive ligand as modulator. Furthermore, the 2D H-MOF nanosheets could be used as a substrate to in situ form and immobilize Pd nanoparticles without any surfactant (denoted as Pd-H-MOF), by means of a simple soaking method of $\mathsf{P d}^{2+}$ precursors. Taking advantages of the gas adsorption capacity of H-MOFs, the surfactant-free Pd catalyst exhibited an excellent catalytic performance.
## Results and discussion
MOF-5 was first selected as an example to prove the conception of competitive coordination strategy. The $2\mathsf{D}\ \mathsf{H}-$ MOF-5 nanosheets were synthesized via a simple solvothermal reaction of zinc acetate $(\mathsf{Z n}(\mathsf{O A c})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})$ , $^{1,4-}$ benzenedicarboxylic acid $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C})$ , lauric acid (LA), and Polyvinylpyrrolidone, K-30 (PVP, K-30) in N,Ndimethylacetamide (DMAC). The porous morphology and structure were identified by diverse characterizations. The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images (Figure 1a, 1b and Figure S1) show a typical shape of 2D H-MOF-5 nanosheets. All the nanosheets spread flatly without curls or wrinkles and the length and width could reach to micron-scale. The porosity of the nanosheets was further confirmed more distinctly by the scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image (Figure 1c) and high-angle annular dark-field scanning TEM (HAADF-STEM) image (Figure 1d), where numerous visible mesopores are throughout the whole nanosheets, even on the edges. High-magnification HAADF-STEM image (inset of Figure 1d) provides an access to better observe the mesopores, which demonstrates that the sizes of pores inside are not so uniform and that the pores form a network of mulDtiOIl:a1y0e.1r0s3l9i/kCe6aSCs0p2o2n72gCe rather than one single layer with pores on it like a paper-cut. The thickness of 2D porous nanosheets was determined to be approximately $19\ \mathsf{n m}$ , using atomic force microscope (AFM) (Figure S2).
As for the composition, elemental mapping by energydispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) shows that the elements C, O, N, Zn are uniformly distributed throughout the whole nanosheet (Figure 1e). And X-ray photoelectron spectrum (XPS) also identifies the composition (Figure S3), where the existence of N is attributed to the residues of solvent molecule and pyrrolidone rings of PVP. Fourier Transform Infrared (FT-IR) spectrum proves the accomplished coordinative interaction between $Z n_{4}O$ clusters and carboxylic group of deprotonated ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B D C}}$ , as disclosed by a red shift in the $c=0$ stretching frequency from $1680~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ - the characteristic $c=0$ stretching frequency of uncoordinated free ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B}}{\mathsf{D}}{\mathsf{C}}^{30}$ - to $1603~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ (Figure S4). The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern of the product, which was obtained after evacuation of solvent molecules at $\boldsymbol{100^{\circ}C}$ under vacuum for $6\mathrm{~h~}$ , corresponds well with that of the simulated MOF-5 XRD patterns31 (Figure S5), while the difference of relative peak intensities might originate from preferential orientation.
The 2D H-MOF-5 nanosheets exhibited high thermal stability $(>400^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}})$ evidenced by the thermogravimetric (TG) curve (Figure S6) taken in air (nitrogen). The weight loss below ${100^{\circ}C}$ could be ascribed to the solvent liberation or the loss of guest molecules, while the significant weight loss in the range from $400^{\circ}\mathsf C$ to $480^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ arises from the framework collapse20, 31. The nanosheets also showed permanent porosity after evacuation of solvent molecules. ${\sf N}_{2}$ sorption data at 77 K displayed a type I isotherm at relatively low $\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{P}_{0}$ pressures for a micropore structure and a typical type IV isotherm at relatively high $\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{P}_{0}^{\ 27}$ (Figure 1f), which is related to the capillary condensation associated with mesopore channels32, suggesting an additional presence of mesopores. The mesopore diameter distribution was calculated using the Barrett-Joyner-Halenda (BJH) method (inset of Figure 1f), indicating a broad range of mesopore diameters, from 6 nm to $30~\mathsf{n m}$ , which could also be confirmed visually by TEM and SEM images. The micropore diameter distribution was also calculated using the Saito-Foley (SF) method32 (Figure S7), revealing the existence of micropores, which are the inherent characteristic of the framework of MOF- ${\boldsymbol{\cdot}}{\boldsymbol{5}}^{1}$ . Due to the hierarchically porous structures, BET specific surface area of the sample could reach up to $\scriptstyle1669\ m^{2}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ .
The formation of these 2D H-MOF-5 nanosheets underwent a morphologic evolution. Small solid square nanosheets came into being at the early stage. Then an etching process took place, with more and more visible pores emerging both inside and on the edges, until they sprawled over the entire nanosheets (Figure 2). The formation of MOF-5 was accomplished at thevery beginning, as confirmed by PXRD (Figure S8) and FT-IR results (Figure S9), and no obvious compositional changes happened during the etching process.
LA could coordinate with carboxyl group to surface-exposed $Z n_{4}0$ sites, acting as a diffusion limiter for both the metal ions and organic ligands, hence controlling crystal growth7. We proposed a competitive coordination mechanism to explain the evolutionary process, in which LA could serve as a competitive ligand or modulator in the coordination to $Z n_{4}0$ clusters33. Deprotonated ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B D C}}$ and LA were both capable of coordinating to $Z n_{4}0$ clusters in the initial stage, though the coordination of deprotonated LA was much weaker, because LA had one single carboxyl group while ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B D C}}$ had two to form the stable MOF networks. As the reaction proceeded, the coordination of deprotonated ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B D C}}$ predominated, while the weak bonding of deprotonated LA to $Z n_{4}O$ clusters dissociated to release LA from the framework. The migration of LA could provide copious sites for further etching and afterwards enable the creation of mesopores in the nanosheets (Figure 3). To confirm the mechanism and disclose the details, a series of control experiments were conducted (Figure S10-S12). It turns out that the final morphology is sensitive to initial concentration and reaction temperature, so these parameters need to be reconsidered when extending to other MOFs. All the reactants are indispensable. $Z n(O A c)_{2}\cdot2H_{2}O$ acts as metallic nodes and ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B D C}}$ as organic linkers to construct the elementary framework of MOF-5. PVP determines the formation of 2D nanosheets, and the molecular weight of PVP plays an important role in directing the morphology (Figure S13). While LA, as mentioned before, directs the etching process and mesoporous structure. Without LA, merely solid nanosheets could be prepared (Figure S12a), because no competitive coordination was involved to provide empty sites for further etching process and the formal coordination led to complete structures. Notably, other long-chain organic acid with 10-18 carbon atoms could function as well (Figure S14) to achieve 2D H-MOF-5 nanosheets, whereas short-chain organic acid or alkane could only result in solid nanosheets (Figure S15).
This competitve coordination strategy is also effective to direct other H-MOFs. By the expansion of MOF-5 structure, other hierarchical-pore IRMOFs, an isoreticular series sharing the same topology with MOF-5, could likewise be achieved. For IRMOF-3, functinalized with amino, H-MOF nanocubes could be prepared through competitive coordination. And for IRMOF-8, extending from the original ligand of the one phenylene ring to two, 2D H-MOF nanosheets could be realized similiar with previous result of MOF-5 (Figure 4). They were further characterized (Figure S16, S17).
